## Solution

**Setup and basic properties.**

Since $f$ is strictly increasing and $f(f(n))=3n$, first note $f(n)>n$ for all $n$: if $f(n)\le n$, then (as $f$ increasing) $f(f(n))\le f(n)\le n<3n$, contradicting $f(f(n))=3n$.

**Determining $f(1)$.** We have $f(f(1))=3$. If $f(1)=1$, then $f(f(1))=f(1)=1\ne 3$. If $f(1)\ge 3$, then $f(f(1))\ge f(3)>f(2)>f(1)\ge 3$, so $f(f(1))\ge 5>3$, contradiction. Hence $f(1)=2$, and then $f(2)=f(f(1))=3$.

**Partition into blocks.** For $k\ge 0$ define
$$A_k=[3^k,\,2\cdot 3^k),\qquad B_k=[2\cdot 3^k,\,3^{k+1}).$$
Note $|A_k|=|B_k|=3^k$ and $\mathbb N^*=\bigsqcup_{k\ge 0}(A_k\cup B_k)$.

**Claim (formula).** The unique solution is:
$$f(n)=n+3^k \quad(n\in A_k),\qquad f(n)=3n-3^{k+1}\quad(n\in B_k).$$

*Verification that $f(f(n))=3n$:* If $n\in A_k$, then $f(n)=n+3^k\in B_k$, so $f(f(n))=3(n+3^k)-3^{k+1}=3n+3^{k+1}-3^{k+1}=3n$. If $n\in B_k$, then $f(n)=3n-3^{k+1}\in[3^{k+1},2\cdot 3^{k+1})=A_{k+1}$, so $f(f(n))=(3n-3^{k+1})+3^{k+1}=3n$. ✓

*Verification that $f$ is strictly increasing:* Within each block $f$ is linear with positive slope. At the internal boundary $n=2\cdot 3^k-1\to n=2\cdot 3^k$: $f(2\cdot3^k-1)=3^{k+1}-1<3^{k+1}=f(2\cdot3^k)$. At the block boundary $n=3^{k+1}-1\to n=3^{k+1}$: $f(3^{k+1}-1)=2\cdot3^{k+1}-3<2\cdot3^{k+1}=f(3^{k+1})$. ✓

**Uniqueness (by induction on $k$).** We show $f$ is forced on each $A_k\cup B_k$.

*Base:* $f(1)=2$ is forced (shown above), giving $f$ on $A_0=\{1\}$; then $f(2)=3$ is forced, giving $f$ on $B_0=\{2\}$.

*Step:* Assume $f$ determined on $A_0\cup B_0\cup\cdots\cup A_k\cup B_k=[1,3^{k+1})$. The map $f:B_k\to A_{k+1}$ is a bijection (it is $f(b)=3b-3^{k+1}$, linear and onto $[3^{k+1},2\cdot3^{k+1})=A_{k+1}$). So every $a\in A_{k+1}$ equals $f(b)$ for a unique $b\in B_k$, and $f(a)=f(f(b))=3b$ is **forced**. Likewise $f:A_{k+1}\to B_{k+1}$ is a bijection ($f(a)=a+3^{k+1}$), so every $b'\in B_{k+1}$ equals $f(a')$ for unique $a'\in A_{k+1}$, and $f(b')=f(f(a'))=3a'$ is **forced**. This determines $f$ on $A_{k+1}\cup B_{k+1}$.

By induction $f$ is uniquely determined on all of $\mathbb N^*$, so $f(2004)$ has a definite value.

**Computation.** We have $3^6=729$, $2\cdot 729=1458$, $3^7=2187$. Since
$$1458\le 2004<2187,$$
we get $2004\in B_6$. Therefore
$$f(2004)=3\cdot 2004-3^7=6012-2187=\boxed{3825}.$$

